<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Cyme (Aeolis)</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 129 BCE (approx.) · capital Cyme</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest of the Aeolian cities on the Anatolian coast, home of Hesiod's father and the historian Ephorus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeolians">Aeolian Greeks</a> founded Cyme on the coast north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediz-river">Hermus</a>, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aliaga">Aliağa</a>, c. 1000 BCE. It became the leading city of the Aeolian dodecapolis, and tradition held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod's</a> father sailed from Cyme to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>. A Cymaean princess was said to have married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">King Midas</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-phrygia">Phrygia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Cyme was a commercial port, minting coins from the 6th century BCE. It fell to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia</a> and then the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>; its tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristagoras-of-cyme">Aristagoras</a> was among those guarding Darius's bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> c. 513 BCE. The city joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a> and later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a>, and the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephorus">Ephorus</a> was born there in the 4th century BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> Cyme passed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamenes">Pergamene</a> control and kept its civic institutions. It came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> when the province of Asia was organised in 129 BCE, and remained a modest city into late antiquity.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cyme-aeolis.jpg" alt="Cyme (Aeolis)" width="320" height="256"><figcaption>Image: Kryston · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Macedonia_and_the_Aegean_World_c.200.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 BCE – 129 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cyme</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Aristagoras</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aeolic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Turkey</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kyme, Aeolian Cyme</dd></dl></aside></div>

